[sliver] This 0.25 mm wide underside is where the local flute backing extends beyond the X18 fixed stop and meets the ordinary curved side wall. The projection is joined to broad wall stock above and beside it; 0.25 mm describes the exposed face width, not the normal section of a thin wall.
alternatives: Extending the fixed stop to X18.25 covers the exposed strip with additional stop material outside its working plane. Continuing the backing to the bed adds stock below the texture field. A thicker full flank removes the local projection at the cost of material throughout the unfluted regions.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=18.125 y=16.000 z=6.000

[sliver] These 0.25 mm strips are the exposed ends of the guide ties: the ties end at X18.25 in magnitude and the stops end at X18. The fore tie retains 3 mm stock between Y2 and Y5; the rear tie retains 3 mm between Y24.5 and Y27.5. Both are bed-rooted and joined to the full guide beam. The small exposed axial width does not make either guide a 0.25 mm plate.
alternatives: Extending the stops outboard to the tie ends hides these strips with added stock outside the motion stop planes. Increasing guide stock to 4 mm inward consumes the running clearance; increasing it outward adds material and reduces the open viewing and cleanup space.
