[ceiling] The Z29.36 face is the actual 3 mm crown underside, including the selected 12 by 6 mm supported reading rectangle. Automatic supports are retained here. The rear opening at X +/-12.25 and Z6..29.36 exposes the crown underside and its supports. The prepared native paths emit 0.48 mm air beneath the ceiling for the requested 0.45 mm support-top distance; geometric access and planned separation do not establish easy physical removal.
alternatives: A corbel replaces the flat supported reading with a different surface. Columns beneath it obstruct the shared slide and support-removal space. More roof stock above the same underside adds material without changing its first-layer support interface.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=0.000 y=29.892 z=29.360

[ceiling] These are the fixed capture-lip undersides at Z7.40. The body-only lint omits the moving shoulders whose tops are Z6.92, so their model-to-model release air is 0.48 mm, not the reported 4.3 mm drop to the fixed floor. Each lip has 3 mm stock up to Z10.40 and is rooted on its 3 mm guide wall. The native project preserves the central release windows. Remove all automatic supports from the empty travel spaces before assessing motion; physical cleanup and release remain unmeasured.
alternatives: A corbel under a lip consumes the release air or the moving stroke. Thickening a lip upward adds material without changing its first release layer. Increasing Z air changes the local fit and belongs to a separate whole-object print.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
